1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,915
April thirteenth, nineteen seventy.

2
00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:11,801
Two hundred thousand miles from Earth, Mission Control gives Apollo thirteen a routine order they have followed a dozen times already: stir the oxygen tanks.

3
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:15,328
Two minutes later, the spacecraft slams sideways.

4
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:19,775
This is the real radio call — not the line from the movie.

5
00:00:19,775 --> 00:00:23,539
Jack Swigert: "Okay, Houston, I believe we've had a problem here."

6
00:00:24,666 --> 00:00:33,652
Fourteen minutes after that, commander Jim Lovell looks out the window and watches his own oxygen boiling away into space — with no way yet to know if any of them will make it home.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:39,305
Everyone thinks they know this story. Almost none of it happened the way the movie says.

8
00:00:39,305 --> 00:00:46,346
This is the actual transcript, the real crew — and the last man alive who was on that spacecraft, because the other two are gone.

9
00:00:47,466 --> 00:00:48,877
You're watching mindwired.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:04,028
This is the real story of Apollo thirteen: the actual transcript behind the movie's most famous lines, the exact wiring failure that nearly killed three men, and what happened to the crew in the fifty-six years since — including two real events from just the last year.

11
00:01:05,166 --> 00:01:08,410
Twelve boys and their coach were never on this flight.

12
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:13,275
Apollo thirteen carried three men: commander Jim Lovell, on his second trip to the Moon.

13
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:16,194
Lunar module pilot Fred Haise, on his first spaceflight.

14
00:01:16,194 --> 00:01:20,735
And command module pilot Jack Swigert, who was not even supposed to be there.

15
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:26,454
Seventy two hours before launch, backup astronaut Charlie Duke came down with German measles.

16
00:01:26,454 --> 00:01:31,369
Prime pilot Ken Mattingly had never had the disease. Lovell and Haise were already immune.

17
00:01:31,369 --> 00:01:36,939
Mattingly was not, so NASA pulled him from his own flight and flew Swigert in his place.

18
00:01:38,066 --> 00:01:39,424
Mattingly never got sick.

19
00:01:39,424 --> 00:01:46,895
Instead, he spent the entire crisis on the ground, helping write the exact procedure that would bring the spacecraft back to life.

20
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:50,617
April eleventh, nineteen seventy. Two thirteen PM.

21
00:01:50,617 --> 00:01:59,109
Apollo thirteen lifts off from Kennedy Space Center, headed for a place no crew had tried to land before: the Fra Mauro highlands.

22
00:02:00,233 --> 00:02:05,650
By this point in the Apollo program, two crews had already landed on the Moon.

23
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:08,178
The public had started calling it routine.

24
00:02:08,178 --> 00:02:12,873
That night, the major television networks did not carry the crew's live broadcast.

25
00:02:12,873 --> 00:02:14,679
They aired regular programming instead.

26
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,711
The mission everyone assumed would be the boring one became the one nobody has ever stopped talking about.

27
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:26,159
Lovell's target wasn't even the flat, easy ground the first two landings used.

28
00:02:26,159 --> 00:02:37,141
Apollo thirteen was aiming for the Fra Mauro highlands, rougher terrain nobody had tried to put a spacecraft down on, because scientists believed it held rock from deep in the Moon's early history.

29
00:02:38,266 --> 00:02:52,477
Two days into the flight, with the crew already past the Moon's halfway point, Mission Control had no idea the tank that would nearly kill all three of them had already failed a ground test three weeks before launch — and been fixed with a shortcut nobody flagged as dangerous.

30
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,946
The failure did not begin in space.

31
00:02:55,946 --> 00:03:01,646
It began two years earlier, on the ground, in a mistake nobody at the time thought mattered.

32
00:03:02,766 --> 00:03:04,180
October nineteen sixty eight.

33
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:08,775
Oxygen tank two is being removed from an earlier spacecraft for a modification.

34
00:03:08,775 --> 00:03:14,077
A bolt is left in place. A lifting fixture breaks. The tank drops two inches.

35
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:17,115
It passes every test afterward.

36
00:03:17,115 --> 00:03:21,330
NASA installs it in the spacecraft that will fly Apollo thirteen.

37
00:03:21,330 --> 00:03:23,245
Nobody knows anything is wrong.

38
00:03:24,366 --> 00:03:28,052
March nineteen seventy, three and a half weeks before launch.

39
00:03:28,052 --> 00:03:33,211
During a routine ground test, oxygen tank two will not fully drain. Not once.

40
00:03:33,211 --> 00:03:38,002
Technicians try again, on different days. It won't go below ninety two percent.

41
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:40,789
So they improvise a fix.

42
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:49,399
They switch on the tank's internal heaters, using ground power at sixty five volts, for eight cumulative hours, to boil the oxygen out through brute force.

43
00:03:50,533 --> 00:03:55,989
The tank's safety thermostat is only rated for twenty eight volts — the normal voltage in flight.

44
00:03:55,989 --> 00:03:58,556
Nobody had ever re-tested it at sixty five.

45
00:03:58,556 --> 00:04:02,728
Under that much power, the switch does not open like it's supposed to.

46
00:04:02,728 --> 00:04:04,012
It welds itself shut.

47
00:04:05,133 --> 00:04:11,655
Inside a sealed, welded tank nobody can see into, the heat destroys the Teflon insulation on the wiring.

48
00:04:11,655 --> 00:04:16,366
The damage is invisible from the outside. The pre-launch review never catches it.

49
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:25,088
Fifty five hours, fifty two minutes into the mission, a routine alarm goes off: low pressure in an unrelated hydrogen tank.

50
00:04:25,088 --> 00:04:30,509
It is nothing serious. But it means Mission Control asks for one more housekeeping task.

51
00:04:31,633 --> 00:04:35,497
"We'd like you to stir up your cryo tanks," Capcom Jack Lousma radios up.

52
00:04:35,497 --> 00:04:39,914
It is a request the crew has carried out dozens of times on this flight alone.

53
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:45,855
Swigert flips the switch. Inside oxygen tank two, the damaged wiring arcs.

54
00:04:45,855 --> 00:04:49,471
It ignites the pure oxygen atmosphere inside the tank.

55
00:04:50,599 --> 00:04:54,929
Fifty five hours, fifty four minutes, fifty three seconds. The tank ruptures.

56
00:04:54,929 --> 00:05:02,145
It blows an entire panel off the side of the spacecraft, and damages the only other oxygen tank on board.

57
00:05:03,266 --> 00:05:07,852
"Okay, Houston, I believe we've had a problem here." That is the real line.

58
00:05:07,852 --> 00:05:15,387
Not "we have a problem." Swigert said it first, in the past tense, and added one word the movie always leaves out: here.

59
00:05:16,533 --> 00:05:22,646
Fifteen seconds later, Lovell repeats it, more precisely: "We've had a Main B Bus Undervolt."

60
00:05:23,766 --> 00:05:28,715
In Mission Control, an engineer named Sy Liebergot is the first to understand what the numbers mean.

61
00:05:28,715 --> 00:05:33,954
"I got a feeling we've lost two fuel cells," he says. "I hate to put it that way."

62
00:05:35,099 --> 00:05:39,515
Within two minutes, the spacecraft loses all three fuel cells and both oxygen tanks.

63
00:05:39,515 --> 00:05:43,615
The command module that was supposed to carry them home is now dying.

64
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:47,924
There is no single voice giving the order to abort.

65
00:05:47,924 --> 00:05:50,478
One flight director wants to turn back immediately.

66
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:59,414
Flight director Gene Kranz and his deputy argue for a few more minutes first — to actually understand what just happened before making a decision they can't undo.

67
00:06:00,533 --> 00:06:05,864
The line NASA is famous for — "failure is not an option" — was never said that night.

68
00:06:05,864 --> 00:06:08,826
It was written twenty five years later, for the movie.

69
00:06:08,826 --> 00:06:14,750
What Kranz actually told his team was different, and just as certain: "I have never lost an American in space.

70
00:06:14,750 --> 00:06:18,897
Sure as hell aren't going to lose one now. This crew is coming home."

71
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:29,064
There is only one part of the spacecraft that still has power, oxygen, and an engine: the lunar module, built to land two men on the Moon for two days.

72
00:06:29,064 --> 00:06:32,075
It now has to keep three men alive for four.

73
00:06:33,199 --> 00:06:36,669
Before the accident, the spacecraft was not even aimed at Earth.

74
00:06:36,669 --> 00:06:42,030
It was on a path meant to reach the Moon and land — not to come home.

75
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:46,131
Without another engine burn, it would miss Earth by nearly three thousand miles.

76
00:06:47,266 --> 00:06:57,950
Lovell manually fires the lunar module's landing engine for thirty five seconds, putting the spacecraft back on a path that will loop the Moon and coast home on gravity alone if nothing else is done.

77
00:06:59,099 --> 00:07:04,004
A second burn, four minutes and twenty four seconds long, speeds the return by ten hours.

78
00:07:04,004 --> 00:07:12,892
Lovell flies it by hand, using the line between day and night on Earth as his only guide, because the guidance computer has been shut off to save power.

79
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:24,800
The man who lost his seat to a measles scare, Ken Mattingly, spends the entire crisis on the ground — helping design the exact power-up sequence that will bring the command module back to life days later.

80
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:30,621
The astronaut who never got to fly ends up being one of the reasons the other three make it home.

81
00:07:31,766 --> 00:07:41,405
Three men breathing in a space built for two creates a new problem: carbon dioxide, climbing toward a lethal level, with the wrong shaped air filters to fix it.

82
00:07:42,533 --> 00:07:54,732
On the ground, a NASA engineer named Ed Smylie and his team build a fix out of what the astronauts already have on board: two square air filters, a plastic bag, cardboard torn from a flight manual, and a roll of tape.

83
00:07:55,866 --> 00:07:59,502
They read the instructions up to the crew, piece by piece.

84
00:07:59,502 --> 00:08:04,460
Swigert builds it by hand, in zero gravity, in a spacecraft that is now freezing.

85
00:08:05,599 --> 00:08:07,088
It works within minutes.

86
00:08:08,233 --> 00:08:12,848
The command module has been powered down and freezing since the night of the explosion.

87
00:08:12,848 --> 00:08:19,309
To survive reentry, it has to be turned back on — using a procedure NASA has never had to write before.

88
00:08:20,433 --> 00:08:25,126
Normally, powering up a spacecraft from a cold stop takes three months of planning.

89
00:08:25,126 --> 00:08:28,479
Mission Control writes this one from scratch in three days.

90
00:08:29,599 --> 00:08:32,569
By now the cabin temperature has dropped near freezing.

91
00:08:32,569 --> 00:08:34,548
Every window is coated in condensation.

92
00:08:34,548 --> 00:08:38,507
The crew has had almost no food or sleep in four days.

93
00:08:39,633 --> 00:08:45,667
Following the new checklist line by line, the crew brings the command module back to life, one system at a time.

94
00:08:46,799 --> 00:08:58,018
By now, the men have barely eaten or slept in four days, in a cabin so cold that Swigert reports the temperature near forty five degrees, every window fogged with condensation.

95
00:08:58,018 --> 00:09:01,637
Nobody on that spacecraft trained for this. Nobody could have.

96
00:09:02,766 --> 00:09:06,020
April seventeenth, nineteen seventy. The spacecraft begins reentry.

97
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:11,715
Radio contact is expected to go silent for three minutes and thirty eight seconds.

98
00:09:11,715 --> 00:09:14,155
It runs almost twice that long.

99
00:09:15,299 --> 00:09:23,110
In Mission Control, nobody says it out loud, but everyone is thinking the same thing: the heat shield, damaged by the explosion, might have failed.

100
00:09:24,233 --> 00:09:27,864
Then, at just over six minutes, the radio comes back.

101
00:09:28,999 --> 00:09:29,695
Parachutes open.

102
00:09:29,695 --> 00:09:34,563
The command module lands in the South Pacific, three miles from the recovery ship.

103
00:09:35,699 --> 00:09:41,941
A helicopter lifts all three men out of the ocean and onto the deck of the USS Iwo Jima.

104
00:09:41,941 --> 00:09:46,540
The radio call back to Houston is four words long: "his condition is excellent."

105
00:09:47,666 --> 00:09:54,197
Eighty seven hours after a routine command triggered an explosion two hundred thousand miles from home, all three men walk away.

106
00:09:55,333 --> 00:10:04,815
NASA convened its own investigation within hours of splashdown: eight people, led by engineer Edgar Cortright, including Apollo eleven's own Neil Armstrong.

107
00:10:05,933 --> 00:10:20,144
Two months later, their report reached one conclusion, in NASA's own words: the accident "was not the result of a chance malfunction, but the result of an unusual combination of mistakes, coupled with a somewhat deficient and unforgiving design."

108
00:10:21,266 --> 00:10:26,100
One dropped tank. One untested voltage. One welded switch. Nobody broke a rule.

109
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:28,330
Nobody was careless in the moment.

110
00:10:28,330 --> 00:10:32,420
The failure was built years before anyone stirred a single tank.

111
00:10:33,566 --> 00:10:35,931
NASA's fix wasn't a warning label.

112
00:10:35,931 --> 00:10:47,359
Every later Apollo command module flew with a third, physically separated oxygen tank, fuel cells that could be individually isolated, and wiring rerouted away from the tank walls entirely.

113
00:10:48,499 --> 00:10:53,253
None of the four crewed Apollo missions that followed ever had another in-flight failure like it.

114
00:10:54,399 --> 00:10:58,095
Twelve years later, Jack Swigert was elected to the United States Congress.

115
00:10:58,095 --> 00:10:59,635
He never took his seat.

116
00:10:59,635 --> 00:11:07,643
He had been diagnosed with bone cancer during the campaign, and died in December nineteen eighty two, one week before he would have been sworn in.

117
00:11:08,766 --> 00:11:11,927
Jim Lovell commanded Apollo thirteen and never flew in space again.

118
00:11:11,927 --> 00:11:16,812
He died at home in Illinois in August of last year, at the age of ninety seven.

119
00:11:17,933 --> 00:11:20,003
"He was our hero," his family said.

120
00:11:20,003 --> 00:11:27,102
"We will miss his unshakeable optimism, his sense of humor, and the way he made each of us feel we could do the impossible."

121
00:11:28,233 --> 00:11:32,036
Fred Haise is now the only member of the crew still alive.

122
00:11:32,036 --> 00:11:40,275
He is ninety two years old, and he still makes public appearances, still answers questions about the mission that nearly killed him fifty six years ago.

123
00:11:41,399 --> 00:11:44,955
None of the three men ever flew in space again after this mission.

124
00:11:44,955 --> 00:11:50,150
For all three of them, the flight that almost killed them was also the last one they ever made.

125
00:11:51,299 --> 00:11:57,020
Two months before he died, Jim Lovell recorded one more message — for a crew that hadn't launched yet.

126
00:11:58,166 --> 00:12:08,432
April sixth of this year, NASA's Artemis two crew flew farther from Earth than any human beings in history — breaking the distance record Apollo thirteen had held for fifty six years.

127
00:12:09,566 --> 00:12:15,250
That same morning, NASA played Lovell's recorded message to the new crew, exactly as he had asked.

128
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:17,925
"Welcome to my old neighborhood," he told them.

129
00:12:19,066 --> 00:12:31,171
A month before that, a documentary called Odyssey premiered at the Tribeca Festival — built from twenty five hundred hours of never-before-seen footage, told entirely through Jim Lovell's own family's perspective.

130
00:12:31,171 --> 00:12:34,295
No narrator. No interviews. Just the real archive.

131
00:12:35,433 --> 00:12:46,195
Fifty six years after Apollo thirteen flew farther than any spacecraft with astronauts on board ever had, a new crew flew past it, carrying a message from a man who wasn't alive to see it happen.

132
00:12:47,333 --> 00:12:58,566
Fifty six years later, the phrase everyone remembers is still wrong, and the record everyone thinks is unbreakable was broken this year, by people carrying the same message the man who set it recorded before he died.

133
00:12:59,699 --> 00:13:02,854
The real transcript said "we've had a problem." Past tense.

134
00:13:02,854 --> 00:13:08,215
And then three men, and thousands of people on the ground, made sure it didn't stay one.

135
00:13:09,333 --> 00:13:19,003
If a crisis with an ending this close still had this much of the real story left out, wait until you see the one where the story everyone thinks they know isn't even the right disaster.

136
00:13:19,003 --> 00:13:21,689
That full story is live on this channel right now.

137
00:13:22,833 --> 00:13:24,557
Subscribe to mindwired.

